WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:02.780
CHANDA: Just tell me

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.839
in very brief--

00:00:04.839 --> 00:00:06.890
how do you define globalization?

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:13.890
FRIEDMAN: Well, I define globalization as the integration of markets, financial systems, telecommunications 

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:14.530
systems,

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.199
and technologies

00:00:16.199 --> 00:00:18.410
in a way that is shrinking the world

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.039
from a size medium to a size small

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:23.739
and enabling each of us, as individuals,

00:00:23.739 --> 00:00:26.299
to reach around the world farther,

00:00:26.299 --> 00:00:27.010
faster,

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:28.490
deeper, and cheaper

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:29.740
than ever before,

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.080
while at the same time enabling the world

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.240
to reach into each of us

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:35.089
farther,

00:00:35.089 --> 00:00:36.880
faster,

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.220
deeper, cheaper than ever before.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.250
CHANDA: Excellent. Very succinct definition, good.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.820
Now move on to

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.570
the interview.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.810
Since

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:54.510
Lexus and the Olive Tree

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.060
was published in 1999,

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.580
the world has changed.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.240
And how do you see this change

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:03.920
has affected globalization

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.280
as a phenomenon?

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.120
FRIEDMAN: Well, you know, the most important

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.030
effect on globalization has clearly been 9/11.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:12.140
It's really been

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.190
a huge a huge "olive tree" event--

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.880
CHADHA: If you could explain what the "olive tree" is--

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.630
FRIEDMAN: A huge assertion of--

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.370
ethnic or religious identity--

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.840
anger, rage--

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.100
into this globalization system.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.689
But because it was targeted on the United States, 

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:35.290
which after all is the heartbeat of the system,

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:40.200
it's slowed down the system a little bit. It's interdicted 

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.890
travel a little bit. We see its impact on the airline industry. 

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:46.200
I won't say it's interdicted trade but it's certainly

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.229
slowed down commerce.

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:50.400
It's put sand in the gears of the system.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.100
Not in a fundamental way,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.340
but the danger

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.150
that 9/11 poses:

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.430
if there's

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.430
another nine eleven

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:02.540
that prompts the United States

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.389
to really

00:02:04.389 --> 00:02:06.340
shrink the apertures 

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.310
into our country--

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:13.309
whether it's the Canadian border or our seaports or airports or rail lines--

00:02:13.309 --> 00:02:17.649
that really could have an effect in slowing down globalization,

00:02:17.649 --> 00:02:20.199
at least for a while. So

00:02:20.199 --> 00:02:21.789
that's the biggest impact--

00:02:21.789 --> 00:02:24.559
the biggest change since the book came out.

00:02:24.559 --> 00:02:28.969
But the other important thing to keep in mind is that there are a lot of misunderstandings about

00:02:28.969 --> 00:02:29.650
this.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:33.040
A lot of people associated the dot-coms with globalization, 

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.249
and so when the dot-coms blew up, people wrote that globalization is over. 

00:02:37.249 --> 00:02:43.410
Now, my answer to them is very simple: it's true that there no longer are five websites 

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:47.790
where you can get 100 pounds of dog food delivered to your house in an hour.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.299
But there's one.

00:02:49.299 --> 00:02:53.769
And that's what's new. That's what you have to keep your eye on. People who say globalization's 

00:02:53.769 --> 00:02:54.679
over.

00:02:54.679 --> 00:02:59.499
CHADHA: And that one is Amazon.com?
FRIEDMAN: Well, there's Amazon.com or one other that we don't know-- 

00:02:59.499 --> 00:03:02.809
dogfood.com or pets.com if it still exists-- 

00:03:02.809 --> 00:03:04.019
but--

00:03:04.019 --> 00:03:06.989
I just used it as an example. But people

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:08.639
who say globalization is over--

00:03:08.639 --> 00:03:11.189
I would like to introduce them to two

00:03:11.189 --> 00:03:13.510
pretty good-sized countries: one's called India,

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:14.989
and one's called China.

00:03:14.989 --> 00:03:17.529
These are two countries that have bet their future

00:03:17.529 --> 00:03:19.689
on a globalization strategy.

00:03:19.689 --> 00:03:24.489
On educating and empowering their people and designing their infrastructure, trade, 

00:03:24.489 --> 00:03:27.969
and governance policies to succeed in globalization. 

00:03:27.969 --> 00:03:30.299
They are two-fifths of the planet.

00:03:30.299 --> 00:03:31.580
Go tell them

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:35.699
that globalization is over.

00:03:35.699 --> 00:03:41.189
CHADHA: One of the features, you said-- the most striking feature of the globalization system, as opposed to the Cold War system,

00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:42.749
is integration.

00:03:42.749 --> 00:03:47.559
Do you see the integration aspect as being somewhat

00:03:47.559 --> 00:03:50.599
hampered by what has happened since 9/11?

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:52.119
FRIEDMAN: There is no question

00:03:52.119 --> 00:03:53.029
that one

00:03:53.029 --> 00:03:54.109
just looks at a

00:03:54.109 --> 00:03:55.509
place like Yale--

00:03:55.509 --> 00:03:57.689
I don't know this, but I assume it--

00:03:57.689 --> 00:03:59.659
that you have foreign students

00:03:59.659 --> 00:04:03.469
that may have come for a semester, gone home, and not been able to come back from spring break 

00:04:03.469 --> 00:04:05.949
because of visa problems. A tiny example.

00:04:05.949 --> 00:04:10.429
But I've had ambassadors around the world writing me about the problems they're having with

00:04:10.429 --> 00:04:11.919
smart, bright kids

00:04:11.919 --> 00:04:14.870
or professionals who have

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:16.649
job opportunities or

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:18.690
study opportunities in the United States

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:22.300
there haven't been able to take them up because of globalization.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.740
CHADHA: That's a tough-- yeah--

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:27.970
FRIEDMAN: As a result, have not been able to take thump as a result of 9/11.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:30.140
That will

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:35.430
slow down globalization if it's permanent. My guess is that there will be technological fixes to

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:36.749
all of these things.

00:04:36.749 --> 00:04:40.330
Some of them may not be pretty, because some of them may involve infringements 

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.560
on civil liberties as we've known them.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.550
But there will be fixes over time. We're in the adjustment period now. 

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:49.709
CHADHA: You know, in one of your

00:04:49.709 --> 00:04:50.530
pieces you wrote 

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:51.930
that Ramzi Yousef,

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.520
the guy who

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.979
blew up the World Trade Center the first time-- 

00:04:55.979 --> 00:04:59.410
his desire to blow up the World Trade Center 

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:01.509
was because,

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:04.300
as you said-- globalization as Americanization 

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.830
had gotten in his face, 

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:07.479
and it had empowered him 

00:05:07.479 --> 00:05:11.080
as an individual to do something about it. 

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:15.139
Now, do you see Americanization and globalization as the same thing?

00:05:15.139 --> 00:05:20.360
FRIEDMAN: I think that it was true to a point and it was certainly true early on, and it's

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.860
less and less true every day.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:26.409
My image of globalization, when one speaks culturally--

00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:30.830
I always ask people, "What's the most popular food in the world?

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.879
Is it the Big Mac?" 

00:05:33.879 --> 00:05:37.029
No, no. "Is it spaghetti?" No, no. 

00:05:37.029 --> 00:05:40.379
The most popular food in the world is pizza, ok? 

00:05:40.379 --> 00:05:43.030
Now why is pizza such a popular food?

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:45.419
Because every culture has a

00:05:45.419 --> 00:05:47.009
flat piece of bread

00:05:47.009 --> 00:05:51.780
on which you can stick all kinds of local
ingredients

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:55.319
and products. So in India you can get curry pizza, 

00:05:55.319 --> 00:05:57.999
and in China you can get sweet and sour pizza, 

00:05:57.999 --> 00:06:01.889
and in Mexico you can get chili pizza or fajita pizza. 

00:06:01.889 --> 00:06:07.419
And I look at kind of the Internet, which is so much of a driver of globalization, 

00:06:07.419 --> 00:06:10.650
as like a big pizza that every society

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:13.169
basically puts its own local culture on.

00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:17.309
I don't know exactly when, but it'll be sometime near 2010,

00:06:17.309 --> 00:06:20.990
where there'll be more Chinese and Indian Internet users 

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:22.700
than there are Americans.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:26.360
And that's going to change the flavor of the Internet pizza.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:27.639
So yes,

00:06:27.639 --> 00:06:30.400
initially America was the driver of this system,

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.740
and dominated in the cultural sense,

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:34.860
but I think it will be less and less true

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.159
with every passing day.

00:06:38.159 --> 00:06:43.839
Chadha:
You also coined this phrase, "superempowered angry men," 

00:06:43.839 --> 00:06:47.159
who are kind of challenging globalization in many ways. 

00:06:47.159 --> 00:06:50.490
But these people are products of globalization themselves, 

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:54.830
but they are trying to create a different kind of globalized world-- is that right?

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:59.259
FRIEDMAN: Well, in some ways, yeah, they're products of globalization in that the hijackers of 

00:06:59.259 --> 00:07:00.819
9/11, some of them

00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:03.740
booked their seats on Travelocity.com.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:08.180
They were very adept at communicating through the Internet, even sending

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:10.319
encoded, embedded messages 

00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:13.419
with stenography as far as we can tell. 

00:07:13.419 --> 00:07:14.779
Cell phones were

00:07:14.779 --> 00:07:17.639
a basic instrument of Osama Bin Laden for 

00:07:17.639 --> 00:07:19.779
passing messages and instructions.

00:07:20.499 --> 00:07:25.539
They were global men in many, many ways. 

00:07:25.539 --> 00:07:27.440
And they have a global vision.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:31.780
I call them Islamo-Leninists, ok? 

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:34.750
Because in another life,

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:40.220
had Osama Bin Ladin or Ayman al-Zawahiri each had been students at Yale,

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:42.589
they would have been Trotskyites or Marxists. 

00:07:42.589 --> 00:07:44.900
These are people with utopian visions. 

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:47.289
They want to bring the kingdom of God on earth.

00:07:47.289 --> 00:07:49.159
But not the kingdom of a race,

00:07:49.159 --> 00:07:50.340
like the Nazis.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:53.049
Not a kingdom of class, like Marxists.

00:07:53.049 --> 00:07:54.639
But a kingdom of a religion,

00:07:54.639 --> 00:07:58.679
Islam.

00:07:58.679 --> 00:08:01.279
CHADHA: And one of the things you said about

00:08:01.279 --> 00:08:04.219
the need for

00:08:04.219 --> 00:08:04.880
fundamentals

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:06.349
that countries need to have

00:08:06.349 --> 00:08:07.879
in order to

00:08:07.879 --> 00:08:10.999
better integrate to the globalized world, like 

00:08:10.999 --> 00:08:12.869
good governance, rule of law, 

00:08:12.869 --> 00:08:14.159
education,

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:17.280
and the stuff you said that cannot be downloaded. 

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:18.209
Which is good.

00:08:18.209 --> 00:08:20.969
The point is, the countries which are

00:08:20.969 --> 00:08:22.360
unfortunate,

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:24.090
to be in a geographically

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:26.949
wrong area

00:08:26.949 --> 00:08:28.810
or economically

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:30.439
not endowed,

00:08:30.439 --> 00:08:33.700
how do you pull up these countries into the globalized world?

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:36.810
FRIEDMAN: Well, it's really

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:40.619
good governance in spades. Now, what do I mean by that?

00:08:40.619 --> 00:08:44.000
the key the globalization

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:46.120
is not in the bandwidth.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.020
It's not in the wires.

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:51.080
It's actually in the good old fundamentals.

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.970
You get your governance right and clean,

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:58.710
get your oversight and regulatory bodies that look over your banking system and financial

00:08:58.710 --> 00:08:59.110
system

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:00.450
in decent shape,

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:04.460
give me a basic rule of law, that as a foreigner I can invest in your country

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:08.860
and feel that if there is a dispute between me and a local businessman, I can get it adjudicated

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:12.550
without having to bribe the judge with a goat, ok?

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:17.560
And you get those basics right and good education systems, so I have a pool of educated people to

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:18.690
draw from--

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.010
and you'll do fine.

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:25.030
You'll do fine if you're Taiwan. Think about Taiwan for a second. This is a barren

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:27.269
rock in a typhoon-laden see

00:09:27.269 --> 00:09:28.960
off the coast of China.

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:30.980
With what, 20 million people on it?

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:33.700
It has the third-largest foreign reserves in the world. 

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:37.820
It doesn't have climate going for it. It doesn't have natural resources going for it--

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:39.510
all it's got going for it:

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:41.210
good fundamentals.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:42.490
So you get your fundamentals right?

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:43.770
The world will find you.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:45.199
The wires will find you.

00:09:45.199 --> 00:09:46.810
The bandwidth will find you.

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:48.090
Get 'em wrong,

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:49.349
and nobody will find you.

00:09:49.349 --> 00:09:51.800
Or nobody will stick with you for very long.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:53.050
So really,

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:54.710
success in globalization,

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:55.899
it's all about

00:09:55.899 --> 00:09:59.570
that good old basic stuff, and that good old basic stuff

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:02.550
is open for any country to acquire.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:05.740
How did Hong Kong succeed?

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:07.040
It succeeded with the basics.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:11.430
Lord knows it has no natural resources.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:16.230
CHADHA: Except the fact that some of the policies of the Western countries, developed countries, 

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:17.790
especially the United States, 

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:21.060
like steel tariffs,

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:23.790
the farm subsidy,

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:25.630
and also

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:28.110
the US attitude towards

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:32.880
the Kyoto Protocol, the International Criminal Court-- 

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:36.400
all this kind of suggests the US is trying to hew its own course. 

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:39.639
At a time when the US is at the cutting edge of globalization

00:10:39.639 --> 00:10:44.070
and the US is the major beneficiary of globalization. So how do you explain this contradiction?

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:45.799
FRIEDMAN: Nayan, you're far too polite. 

00:10:45.799 --> 00:10:49.790
It's not just a contradiction, it's a shameful 

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:50.190
contradiction.

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:51.900
It's wanting to have it both ways. 

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:55.350
It's wanting to force open other people's markets 

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:57.850
for what we make and export

00:10:57.850 --> 00:10:58.379
successfully

00:10:58.379 --> 00:11:00.530
while keeping our markets closed

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:05.650
to some of the things they make and export successfully-- often food and textiles for developing

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:06.200
countries.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:07.860
It's wrong.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:11.050
It's a travesty. And it's the great Achilles' heel 

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:15.130
of the globalization system. Someone told me the other day-- this might not be accurate-- 

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:17.209
but a friend of mine from India told me that

00:11:17.209 --> 00:11:21.010
there was a study done about the farm subsidies for other countries,

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:25.190
and if you took all those subsidies and put them together,

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:29.209
you could fly every cow around the world first class.

00:11:29.209 --> 00:11:31.120
Ok? So

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:35.550
I don't know if that's accurate, but I bet the direction is roughly right.

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:37.930
These are political questions.

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:40.430
Hopefully we'll eventually get over them.

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:43.740
I'm all for buying off steelworkers;

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:48.060
I'm all for buying off our farmers. Let's cushion them from the ravages.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:49.810
But what I'm not for

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:51.930
is being stupid

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:57.140
and when someone is knocking on my door as a country and saying, "We'd like to sell you

00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:00.870
 steel for less than it costs us to make it," 

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:04.660
and we slam the door and say, "No no no, we don't want that"--

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:06.009
that's just stupid,

00:12:06.009 --> 00:12:09.309
and that's what retards your growth as a country, so

00:12:09.309 --> 00:12:12.770
we all need to take advantage of the system, but we need to make it fair,

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:15.500
and there I believe the third world,  the developing world has a big and

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:18.190
and justifiable complaint.

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:21.589
CHADHA: And that complaint, of course, is being expressed mostly 

00:12:21.589 --> 00:12:24.630
at the WTO forum.
FRIEDMAN: Yes.

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:25.900
CHADHA: And

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:28.510
do you have any thoughts as to

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:30.590
what should one do about

00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:34.060
the intellectual property issue in WTO,

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:34.830
which is becoming a measure

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.580
of struggle in

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:37.470
global trade?

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:42.400
FRIEDMAN: I don't know enough about where the proper compromise should be there. But what I know is

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:46.800
this: people want to talk about whether we globalize, whether we should globalize-- 

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:50.270
them, I don't have much patience for. Because

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:52.890
globalization here is being driven by technology,

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:54.950
and this is the wave of the future.

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:57.400
But people want to talk about how we globalize-- 

00:12:57.400 --> 00:12:59.480
how we do it technologically,

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:00.910
how we do it legally,

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:04.410
how we do it in terms of trade, and how we make it fair? 

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:07.650
That's a discussion I'm ready to have all day long. 

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:12.250
So I would have to defer to the experts on where the right compromise is

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:15.140
on issues like patents, but

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:19.440
it's certainly a discussion that we should be having.  

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:21.040
CHADHA: And finally,

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:25.600
as you know, the [incoherent] theory of globalization in the late nineteenth

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:26.830
century

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:28.140
has abruptly ended. 

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:29.540
The beginning of twentieth, 

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:31.350
with the

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:35.810
 tariff walls rising and immigration barrier rising-- 

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:37.010
do you see this,

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:41.770
the same phenomenon, reproducing again now with what's happening in Europe vis-a-vis 

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:44.560
immigrants? Even in this country, about 

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:46.810
immigrants from southern countries being 

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:49.370
more difficult to receive? 

00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:50.269
How do you see this?

00:13:50.269 --> 00:13:55.180
FRIEDMAN: Well, you know, the first era of globalization was really built around a fall in transportation

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:55.860
costs

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.160
thanks to the steamship and the railroad.

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:00.999
This era of globalization is built

00:14:00.999 --> 00:14:05.070
on something different; it's built on falling telecommunications costs

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:08.520
and the transfer of voice and data and information

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:10.670
in an era of service economies.

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:12.830
I see it being hampered, potentially,

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:14.300
by the rise of

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:17.770
this terrorist phenomena and how it could

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:20.180
put sand in the gears of the system.

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:25.670
But unless you tell me that people are ready to accept radically lower standards of living,

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:27.590
I don't think

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:29.920
we will consciously,

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:32.140
as governments and societies,

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:33.620
want to go back

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:36.440
to the pre-globalization era. 

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:40.720
Now, it may be that there's going to be a transition here where we find the technological ways. You know, I was just

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:42.000
at the Davos

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.130
World Economic Forum, and when you go there as a participant, 

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:46.400
they give you a name-

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:50.610
tag which you wear around your neck, and it has your picture and barcode on it, 

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:54.940
and you can go to a computer and get all your messages, and you can even-- 

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:57.640
when you go in and out of the Davos Conference Center, 

00:14:57.640 --> 00:14:58.889
you press this,

00:14:58.889 --> 00:15:02.850
and it gets you entry, and your picture and your whole bio comes up.

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:04.790
We may be going to that system. 

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:06.600
Certainly if there's another 9/11, 

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:09.690
we're all going to be wearing Davos badges around our neck

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:12.429
to get into the airport, to get into the sports stadium,

00:15:12.429 --> 00:15:14.490
to get into any public place.

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.690
That is, alas, I fear where we will go. 

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:24.690
But where we won't go, I believe, is backwards to some prehistoric age. 

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:26.770
CHADHA: In the last

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:29.819
several months, developments in Latin America, especially 

00:15:29.819 --> 00:15:30.800
Argentina,

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:34.060
Brazil, Peru, 

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:36.170
are

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:37.780
leading people to say,

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:40.900
"Maybe Latin America is gonna sign off on globalization." 

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:41.859
Do you see that

00:15:41.859 --> 00:15:43.570
as a possibility?

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:49.280
FRIEDMAN: I don't see it at all. I see Latin America, I'm afraid, in my golden straitjacket, 

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:54.370
struggling to find a way to succeed in this system

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:58.270
consistent with its own culture. The reason globalization is going on so successfully,

00:15:58.270 --> 00:15:59.270
I think,

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:02.790
is more and more countries are learning how to globalize, 

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:04.630
how to

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:09.050
fit their own culture, society, and social needs into the demands

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:12.390
of the global market. And the countries that are 

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:15.120
doing the best, I think, are countries like India, 

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:18.440
which hasn't opened up its markets fully--

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:20.230
or China, 

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:21.880
which has gone slowly

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:22.559
into this

00:16:22.559 --> 00:16:24.770
but at the same time moved ahead. I always say,

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:27.760
in this globalization system, there's just one road; 

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:31.000
folks, there's just one road, but when someone comes and says they've found a whole new road 

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:31.939
to prosperity,

00:16:31.939 --> 00:16:36.660
oh, I grab my wallet. I know I came in here with fifty dollars, and I'm leaving with fifty dollars. 

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:39.379
There's just one road, and it's the road of,

00:16:39.379 --> 00:16:41.300
I believe, free markets,

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:43.330
or liberal markets, liberalized markets, 

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:45.810
and liberalized politics.

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:47.900
But there are many speeds, Nayan. 

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:51.230
There's one road, but there's many speeds, and every country

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:52.470
should go down the road

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:54.940
in a way that's consistent with

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:56.740
maintaining its cultural cohesion,

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:58.249
its social cohesion,

00:16:58.249 --> 00:17:02.030
but at the same time its economic development. For some, it may be 500 miles an hour; 

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:04.069
for others it may be 50.

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:06.520
But promise me you just won't do one thing:

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:08.020
not go down the road at all.

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:09.049
If you do that,

00:17:09.049 --> 00:17:10.560
I promise you,

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:15.209
you'll bring nothing but ruin and devastation your people.

00:17:15.209 --> 00:17:15.840
CHADHA: Excellent. Thank you very much. Thank you.
FRIEDMAN: My pleasure. 

